! HeH+ / STO-3G, R = 1.5 bohr: TDDFT dipole-moment (GMH) analysis and
! excited-state export regression  (v0.87.1)
!
! HeH+ is the smallest charge-transfer-like system: the CIS excitation
! moves the electron pair density from He toward H+, flipping the
! dipole moment.  Every number below is verified against an
! INDEPENDENT hand-coded reference (python RHF + CIS with s-orbital
! ERIs and the code's own STO-3G exponents; see
! BENCHMARK_NOTES_v0.87.1.md):
!
!   RHF total energy:            -2.845257812576 Ha
!   CIS singlet excitation:       1.0653318 Ha = 28.9892 eV
!   f_osc:                        0.5532
!   Ground-state dipole mu_0:     ( 0, 0,  1.151257 ) au
!   Excited-state dipole mu_11:   ( 0, 0, -0.106735 ) au  (UNRELAXED)
!   Transition dipole |mu_01|:      0.882600 au  (sign phase-arbitrary)
!   GMH coupling H_01 [Cave-Newton, CPL 249, 15 (1996)]:
!       dE = 1.065332 Ha, |dmu| = 1.257993 au, mu_tr" = 0.882600 au
!       H_01 = 0.43378072 Ha = 11.8038 eV
!
! NOTE (a good teaching point): state dipoles of a CHARGED system
! depend on the coordinate origin (here: He at the origin, H on +z at
! 1.5 bohr); dipole DIFFERENCES and transition dipoles entering the
! GMH formula are origin-independent only for neutral systems, so for
! ions the whole analysis must be quoted with its origin, as done in
! the output header.
!
! %export_tddft writes:
!   heh (this input's basename) + _tddft_mo.molden -- ground-state MOs
!       (standard MOLDEN: [Atoms] AU / [GTO] / [MO], Cartesian);
!   ... + _tddft_states.txt -- Gaussian-style excitation blocks:
!       "Excited State   1: Singlet-A  28.9892 eV ... f= 0.5532"
!       "   1 ->   2    0.70711"       (X/sqrt(2); sum c^2 = 0.5)
!   for post-processing with MultiWfn / THEODORE-class tools.
%method  HF
%basis   sto-3g
%job     energy
%charge  1
%mult    1
%tddft   1
%tddft_tda on
%tddft_dipmom on
%export_tddft on
%geom angstrom
He  0.0  0.0  0.0
H   0.0  0.0  0.793765816355
*end
